msgid "Dev UI for extension developers"
msgstr ""

msgid "Dev UI v2"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This guide covers the Dev UI v2, which is the default from Quarkus 3 onwards."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This guide covers the Quarkus Dev UI for xref:building-my-first-extension."
"adoc[extension authors]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus ships with a Developer UI, which is available in dev mode (when you "
"start\n"
"quarkus with `mvn quarkus:dev`) at http://localhost:8080/q/dev-ui[/q/dev-ui] "
"by default. It will show you something like\n"
"this:"
msgstr ""

msgid "It allows you to:"
msgstr ""

msgid "quickly visualize all the extensions currently loaded"
msgstr ""

msgid "view extension statuses and go directly to extension documentation"
msgstr ""

msgid "view and change `Configuration`"
msgstr ""

msgid "manage and visualize `Continuous Testing`"
msgstr ""

msgid "view `Dev Services` information"
msgstr ""

msgid "view the Build information"
msgstr ""

msgid "view and stream various logs"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each extension used in the application will be listed. There, you can find "
"the guides, additional information, and the applicable configuration for "
"each extension:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Make my extension extend the Dev UI"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To make your extension listed in the Dev UI, you don't need to do anything!"
msgstr ""

msgid "So you can always start with that :)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Extensions can:"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<add-links-to-an-extension-card,Add links to an extension card>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<add-full-pages,Add full custom pages>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<add-a-footer-tab,Add a footer tab>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<add-a-section-menu,Add a section menu>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<custom-cards,Create a custom card>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "Add links to an extension card"
msgstr ""

msgid "External links"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"These are links that reference other (external from Dev UI) data. This data "
"can be HTML pages, text, or other data."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A good example of this is the SmallRye OpenAPI extension that contains links "
"to the generated OpenAPI schema in both JSON and YAML format, and a link to "
"Swagger UI:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The links to these external references are known at build time. So to get "
"links like this on your card, you add the following Build Step in your "
"extension:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Always make sure that this build step is only run when in dev mode"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To add anything on the card, you must return/produce a `CardPageBuildItem`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To add a link, you can use the `addPage` method, as all links go to a "
"\"page\". `Page` has some builders to assist with building a page. For "
"`external` links, use the `externalPageBuilder`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Adding the url of the external link (in this case, we use "
"`NonApplicationRootPathBuildItem` to create this link, as this link is under "
"the configurable non-application path, default `/q`). Always use "
"`NonApplicationRootPathBuildItem` if your link is available under `/q`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can (optionally) hint the content type of the content you are navigating "
"to. If there is no hint, a header call will be made to determine the "
"`MediaType`;"
msgstr ""

msgid "You can add an icon. All free font-awesome icons are available."
msgstr ""

msgid "Note about icons"
msgstr ""

msgid "Embedding external content"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, even external links will render inside (embedded) in Dev UI. In "
"the case of HTML, the page will be rendered, and any other content will be "
"shown using https://codemirror.net/[code-mirror] to markup the media type. "
"For example, the OpenAPI schema document in YAML format:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you do not want to embed the content, you can use the `.doNotEmbed()` on "
"the Page Builder, this will then open the link in a new tab."
msgstr ""

msgid "Runtime external links"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The example above assumes you know the link to use at build time. There "
"might be cases where you only know this at runtime. In that case, you can "
"use a <<JsonRPC>> Method that returns the link to add, and use that when "
"creating the link. Rather than using the `.url` method on the page builder, "
"use the `.dynamicUrlJsonRPCMethodName(\"yourJsonRPCMethodName\")`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Adding labels"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can add an option label to the link in the card using one of the builder "
"methods on the page builder. These labels can be"
msgstr ""

msgid "static (known at build time) `.staticLabel(\"staticLabelValue\")`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"dynamic (loaded at runtime) `."
"dynamicLabelJsonRPCMethodName(\"yourJsonRPCMethodName\")`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"streaming (continuously streaming updated values at runtime) `."
"streamingLabelJsonRPCMethodName(\"yourJsonRPCMethodName\")`"
msgstr ""

msgid "For dynamic and streaming labels, see the <<JsonRPC>> Section."
msgstr ""

msgid "Add full pages"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also link to an \"internal\" page (as opposed to the above "
"\"external\" page). This means that you can build the page and add data and "
"actions for rendering in Dev UI."
msgstr ""

msgid "Build time data"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To make build time data available on your full page, you can add any data to "
"your `CardPageBuildItem` with a key and a value:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can add multiple of these key-value pairs for all the data you know at "
"build time that you need on the page."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"There are a few options to add full page content in Dev UI. Starting from "
"the most basic (good start) to a full blown web-component (preferred)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Display some build time data on a screen (without having to do frontend "
"coding):"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you have some data that is known at build time that you want to display, "
"you can use one of the following builders in `Page`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<raw-data,Raw data>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<table-data,Table data>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<qute-data,Qute data>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "<<web-component-page,Web Component page>>"
msgstr ""

msgid "Raw data"
msgstr ""

msgid "This will display your data in its raw (serialised) JSON value:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Use the `rawDataPageBuilder`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Link back to the key used when you added the build time data in "
"`addBuildTimeData` on the Page BuildItem."
msgstr ""

msgid "That will create a link to a page that renders the raw data in JSON:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Table data"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also display your Build time data in a table if the structure allows "
"it:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Use the `tableDataPageBuilder`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Optionally only show certain fields."
msgstr ""

msgid "That will create a link to a page that renders the data in a table:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Qute data"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also display your build time data using a qute template. All build "
"time data keys are available to use in the template:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Use the `quteDataPageBuilder`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Link to the Qute template in `/deployment/src/main/resources/dev-ui/`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use any Qute template to display the data, for example, `qute-jokes-template."
"html`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`jokes` added as a build time data key on the Page Build Item."
msgstr ""

msgid "Web component page"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To build an interactive page with actions and runtime (or build time) data, "
"you must use the web component page:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Use the `webComponentPageBuilder`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Link to the Web Component in `/deployment/src/main/resources/dev-ui/`. The "
"title can also be defined (using `.title(\"My title\")` in the builder), but "
"if not, the title will be assumed from the componentLink, which should "
"always have the format `qwc` (stands for Quarkus Web Component) dash "
"`extensionName` (example, `arc` in this case ) dash `page title` (\"Beans\" "
"in this case)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Dev UI uses https://lit.dev/[Lit] to make building these web components "
"easier. You can read more about Web Components and Lit:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://www.webcomponents.org/introduction[Web components Getting started]"
msgstr ""

msgid "https://lit.dev/docs/[Lit documentation]"
msgstr ""

msgid "Basic structure of a Web component page"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A Web component page is just a JavaScript Class that creates a new HTML "
"Element:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can import Classes and/or functions from other libraries.\n"
"In this case, we use the `LitElement` class and `html` & `css` functions "
"from `Lit`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Build time data as defined in the Build step and can be imported using the "
"key and always from `build-time-data`. All keys added in your Build step "
"will be available."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The component should be named in the following format: Qwc (stands for "
"Quarkus Web Component), then Extension Name, then Page Title, all "
"concatenated with Camel Case. This will also match the file name format as "
"described earlier. The component should also extend `LitComponent`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"CSS styles can be added using the `css` function, and these styles only "
"apply to your component."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Styles can reference globally defined CSS variables to make sure your page "
"renders correctly, especially when switching between light and dark mode. "
"You can find all CSS variables in the Vaadin documentation (https://vaadin."
"com/docs/latest/styling/lumo/lumo-style-properties/color[Color], https://"
"vaadin.com/docs/latest/styling/lumo/lumo-style-properties/size-space[Sizing "
"and Spacing], etc.)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Properties can be added. Use `_` in front of a property if that property is "
"private. Properties are usually injected in the HTML template, and can be "
"defined as having state, meaning that if that property changes, the "
"component should re-render. In this case, the beans are Build time data and "
"only change on hot-reload, which will be covered later."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Constructors (optional) should always call `super` first and then set the "
"default values for the properties."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The render method (from `LitElement`) will be called to render the page. In "
"this method, you return the markup of the page you want. You can use the "
"`html` function from `Lit`, which gives you a template language to output "
"the HTML you want. Once the template is created, you must only set or change "
"the properties to re-render the page content. Read more about https://lit."
"dev/docs/components/rendering/[Lit html]"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can use the built-in template functions to do conditional, list, etc. "
"Read more about https://lit.dev/docs/templates/overview/[Lit Templates]"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You must always register your Web component as a custom element, with a "
"unique tag. Here, the tag will follow the same format as the filename (`qwc` "
"dash `extension name` dash `page title` );"
msgstr ""

